// block/psu-01/console — a diagnostic port: USB-C in, a UART out, and a barrier between them so
// a laptop never touches the star's return. Ported from arc's `block::psu_01::console`.
//
// Three parts in a row. The USB-C sheet, data-only — receptacle, Rd on both CC pins, the ESD
// array, and no blocking leg, because nothing on this board runs off the cable. A CP2102N bridge
// in its bus-powered arrangement, which *does* run off the cable: VBUS into its regulator, and the
// 3.3 V it makes is the island's rail. And an ISO6721B with one channel each way, its far side on
// that island rail and its host side on the board's 3.3 V. The MCU sees a UART; the laptop sees a
// serial port; the cable's shell and the board's ground never meet.
//
// Why a bridge and not a peripheral: the C092 is the C0 die with FDCAN where the C071 has USB,
// and there is no variant with both. Why an island: this is the one node whose ground *is* the
// backbone's return, and a laptop on mains puts that return on the building's earth through the
// cable's shell — the exact loop every other node's barrier exists to break, arriving from the
// outside. No part on the island is powered by the board, so with the cable out the island is
// simply dark, and the barrier's default-high output is what the MCU's RXD reads then: an idle
// line, not a break. That is the B in ISO6721B.
//
// What the port is testing:
//
// - **Two returns that never meet, said by two power ports on one part.** The isolator's `host`
//   and `far` map different ground pins, and nothing in this block joins them: the island is a
//   second net set, and its name says whose it is.
// - **A UART crossed twice, once per link.** The bridge's serial side meets the isolator's far
//   face in one `connect`, the MCU meets the host face in another (on the board), and the type's
//   `match` does each crossover — so the block's `uart` is the host face re-exported line by
//   line, never a third `connect`.

use "@stackup/passives"
use "@stackup/types"
use "@stackup/bus/usb/cp2102n"
use "@stackup/isolator/iso6721"
use "../../shared/usb.kdl"

block console {
    // The board's 3.3 V — the isolator's host side, and the only thing on this sheet that is on
    // the board's ground. What the sheet demands.
    port logic type=power
    // The serial side, at the barrier: the sheet transmits on the isolator's OUTA and receives on
    // its INB. The MCU's link crosses them over.
    port uart type=uart

    // The port. The sheet mints the cable's rail on the cable's own return, and nothing here ties
    // that return to the board; the island begins at the shell. USB on the silk.
    place usb-port port
    circuit port.vbus.rail name="VBUS"
    circuit port.vbus.gnd name="USB_GND"

    // The bridge, off the cable: VBUS into its regulator, its UART out on the 3.3 V it makes. The
    // cable's Rd is what makes a source put 5 V on the pair at all, and the sense divider on the
    // bridge is what tells it the cable is there.
    place cp2102n bridge vbus=port.vbus designator=USB_UART
    connect usb from=bridge to=port
    circuit bridge.vdd.rail name="3V3_USB"

    // The barrier: one channel each way, the far side on the bridge's 3.3 V and the near side on
    // the board's. The island's link is joined from the isolator's far face to the bridge's
    // serial side, and the host face is this block's `uart`.
    place iso6721b iso host=logic far=bridge.vdd designator=USB_ISO
    connect uart from=iso.far-uart to=bridge.uart
    circuit uart.tx iso.host-uart.tx
    circuit uart.rx iso.host-uart.rx

    // What the bridge carries and the board does not spend. The modem-control inputs have weak
    // pull-ups on the die; the outputs drive nothing; the GPIO are unconfigured. DTR/RTS are not
    // wired to reset and boot: the C0 keeps boot selection in option bytes, and entering the ROM
    // later is firmware's rather than a modem line's.
    nc bridge.RI bridge.CTS bridge.DSR bridge.DCD \
        note="modem-control inputs — weak pull-ups on the die, no peer to drive them"
    nc bridge.RTS bridge.DTR bridge.SUSPEND bridge.SUSPENDB \
        note="modem-control and suspend outputs — the console carries data only"
    nc bridge.GPIO0 bridge.GPIO1 bridge.GPIO2 bridge.GPIO3 \
        note="GPIO — unconfigured; the bridge's job here is the one UART"
    nc bridge.NC note="the part's own NC"
}
